    <main class="sheet" id="paper">
      <div class="title-block">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> MEU EXCHANGE · TECHNICAL WHITEPAPER</span>
        <h1>Gold and silver credit,<br />settled on proofs.</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">A lending and dealing desk on Creditcoin where Chainlink price and Proof-of-Reserve feeds are carried from Ethereum as Merkle inclusion proofs and verified by the block-prover precompile inside the same transaction that acts on them.</p>
        <div class="title-meta">
          <span>Creditcoin CC3 Testnet · chain 102031</span>
          <span>Attestcoin Protocol readability</span>
          <span>Version 1.0</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="abstract">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> ABSTRACT</span>
        <p style="margin-top:20px">Tokenised real-world assets inherit a trust problem their collateral does not have. The metal sits in an audited vault and its price is published by an independent feed, but the chain that lends against them can read neither. Every production answer routes both facts through an operator who <em>writes</em> them onto the lending chain, and that operator — not the vault, not the auditor, not the feed — becomes the thing a lender actually trusts.</p>
        <p>MEU Exchange removes the writer. Four independently operated Chainlink feeds on Ethereum mainnet — two metal prices, two Proof-of-Reserve attestations — and a collateral escrow on Ethereum Sepolia are carried to Creditcoin by the Attestcoin Protocol as Merkle inclusion proofs bound to attested source blocks. Creditcoin's block-prover precompile verifies each proof natively, synchronously, in the same transaction as the business logic that consumes it. The contracts are written so that a missing or stale proof is not a degraded display but a revert: issuance beyond proved reserves, a draw before the pledge is proved, and a fill against an unrefreshed round each fail with a named error.</p>
        <p>This paper describes the venue, the transport, the contract model, the listing path and the risk parameters, and then states plainly what the construction does not achieve. It proves provenance, not truth: that a named feed published a number, not that the number is correct. The auditor remains trusted. That boundary is argued here as a feature of an honest design rather than smoothed over, because a proof system oversold is worse than one honestly bounded.</p>
      </div>

      <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> CONTENTS</span>
        <ol>
          {sections.map(([num, title, id]) => (
            <li><a href={`#${id}`}><span class="num">{num}</span><span>{title}</span></a></li>
