      <section class="chapter" id="problem">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> SECTION 1</span>
        <h2>The trust gap in on-chain RWA credit</h2>
        <p>A desk that lends against bullion has to answer three questions, and a blockchain is the wrong instrument for all three. <strong>Is the collateral really escrowed?</strong> The escrow lives on whichever chain the tokenised metal was issued on. <strong>What is the metal worth right now?</strong> The reference price is published by a feed operating on a chain chosen for liquidity, not for lending. <strong>Are the issuer's reserves real?</strong> That is an auditor's finding, published as a Proof-of-Reserve feed somewhere else again.</p>
        <p>Each answer exists, on a chain, in a form a contract could in principle check. None of them is visible from the chain that needs them. The standard remedy is a relay: an operator observes the source chain and writes the observed value into a contract on the destination chain. The value is now readable, and the guarantee has quietly changed. The lender no longer trusts the auditor and the feed; they trust the operator's willingness and ability to report both faithfully, and the compromise of one key now moves a book.</p>
        <h3>Displaying a proof is not the same as requiring one</h3>
        <p>A weaker version of the same gap appears in systems that do carry attested data but treat it as decoration. Attested figures are rendered in an interface while the contract's state transitions run on separately written values, so the proof is evidence offered to a human rather than a precondition enforced by code. The distinction is observable: if the proof is missing or stale, does the system show a warning, or does the transaction fail?</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <span class="callout-label">THE DESIGN RULE</span>
          <p>Every state transition that moves value in this system is gated on a proof verified in the same transaction. Where a proof is absent, stale, replayed, or emitted by an unbound address, the call reverts with a named error rather than proceeding on a lesser guarantee. Section 9 shows each of those refusals executed on a live testnet, paired with the transaction that then succeeded.</p>
        </div>
        <p>Three properties follow from that rule, and they are the claims this paper defends. Supply cannot exceed reserves that a Chainlink Proof-of-Reserve feed has published and this chain has verified. Credit cannot be drawn against collateral whose escrow has not been proved from the chain holding it. A trade cannot fill at a price no one has re-proved inside the staleness bound.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="creditcoin">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> SECTION 2</span>
        <h2>Creditcoin as the venue</h2>
        <p>Creditcoin is an EVM-compatible layer-1 blockchain built with the Polkadot SDK, whose documentation describes it as the evolution of an earlier Substrate-based credit protocol into a chain where "developers can build credit applications using familiar Ethereum tools." <sup><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup> The name now refers specifically to the EVM-compatible network; the original Substrate protocol is documented separately as CC Enterprise.</p>
        <h3>Consensus and execution</h3>
        <p>The network runs Nominated Proof-of-Stake. The EVM layer has no separate consensus of its own — it inherits block production and finalisation from the Substrate runtime, with an EVM built on Frontier. <sup><a href="#ref-3">[3]</a></sup> The published specification gives a 15-second block time and finality in one to three blocks, roughly 350 transactions per second with the EVM in use and roughly 1,400 without, a 75,000,000 block gas limit and a 0.5 gwei base gas price. <sup><a href="#ref-2">[2]</a></sup></p>
        <p>One difference from Ethereum matters when reasoning about the cost of proof submission. Creditcoin prices transactions using the Substrate notion of <em>weight</em> rather than Ethereum's gas accounting, with the results "designed to produce effectively identical" outcomes. <sup><a href="#ref-3">[3]</a></sup> In practice this surfaces as gas estimation that does not always report a precompile revert the way an Ethereum node would — a detail the proof worker in Section 5 has to handle explicitly rather than assume away.</p>
        <h3>Why a credit desk belongs here</h3>
        <p>Two properties make this venue a fit for the design rather than an arbitrary host.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Cross-chain verification is native, not a contract.</strong> Creditcoin's Universal Smart Contracts let a contract "coordinate, interact with, and update smart contracts across multiple blockchains." <sup><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup> The verification step is a precompile in the runtime, so a proof is checked by the same node software that validates the block. There is no bridge contract holding a multisig, and no separate validator set to compromise, between the evidence and the contract consuming it.</li>
          <li><strong>Finality is fast enough to gate on.</strong> With 15-second blocks and finality inside three, a proof submitted when a price round lands is actionable within roughly the same window a conventional oracle push would take. Synchronous verification therefore costs latency measured against attestation lag, not against the chain.</li>
          <li><strong>The chain's purpose is credit.</strong> Creditcoin exists to carry lending applications, so a repo desk is a first-class use of the venue rather than a general-purpose contract deployed onto indifferent infrastructure.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Everything in this paper executes on <strong>Creditcoin CC3 Testnet</strong>, chain ID 102031, native currency tCTC. Ethereum mainnet is read-only throughout: the Chainlink feeds are the real mainnet feeds and nothing in this system writes to them. Ethereum Sepolia holds the collateral escrow.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="attestcoin">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> SECTION 3</span>
        <h2>The Attestcoin readability model</h2>
        <p>The Attestcoin Protocol is the cross-chain layer hosted on Creditcoin. Its documentation splits the problem into two stages: <em>attestation</em>, described as "proactively tracking and reaching consensus on the state changes of source blockchains", and <em>transaction proving</em>, in which a specific transaction containing the data a builder wants is proved on demand. <sup><a href="#ref-4">[4]</a></sup></p>
        <h3>Attestation</h3>
        <p>A set of attestors "listen for new source chain blocks, vote on attestations, and store those attestations on-chain." <sup><a href="#ref-4">[4]</a></sup> This runs continuously and independently of any application. By the time a contract wants to read something, the block containing it has typically already been attested — the work of agreeing what a source chain's history is has been done ahead of the request, not in response to it.</p>
        <p>Attestation is not instantaneous. Coverage trails the source chain's head, so a freshly emitted event is not immediately provable. In this system that lag is visible rather than hidden: the dashboard deliberately renders a market spot reference beside the proved round so that the gap between them is on screen, and the gap is attestation lag.</p>
        <h3>Proving and verification</h3>
        <p>Proofs are generated off-chain and checked on-chain — the documentation is explicit that this is a compute trade-off: "to save on-chain compute, we generate proofs off-chain then verify them on-chain," with builders requesting proofs of the transaction containing a target event from a proof generation server. <sup><a href="#ref-4">[4]</a></sup> Two proofs travel together:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A <strong>Merkle inclusion proof</strong> placing the transaction inside a specific source block.</li>
          <li>A <strong>continuity proof</strong> anchoring that block to the attestation record already stored on Creditcoin.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The <strong>block-prover precompile</strong> at <code>0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000FD2</code> "verifies merkle and continuity proofs, signaling whether or not the source chain transaction is valid." <sup><a href="#ref-4">[4]</a></sup> Because it is a precompile, the check happens inside the transaction that needs it. There is no window in which unverified data sits in contract storage waiting to be validated, because state is only written after the precompile has returned.</p>
        <h3>ASCBase, and what an application must implement</h3>
        <p>An Attestcoin Smart Contract inherits one entry point. <code>ASCBase.execute</code> computes a query id from the chain key, block height and Merkle proof, rejects any id already processed, calls the precompile, and only then hands control to the application's <code>_processAndEmitEvent</code>.</p>
        <pre><code set:html={executeSignature}></code></pre>
        <p>Deduplication by query id is a protocol-level guarantee, and a load-bearing one: a replayed proof is still a <em>valid</em> proof. Without the check, anyone could resubmit yesterday's price round and roll the book back to it. The applications here add a second, independent guard in the same spirit — round ids must move strictly forward — because the same source transaction can be reached by more than one route.</p>
        <p>Inside the handler, proved bytes are decoded with <code>EvmV1Decoder</code>, which exposes receipt fields and log entries filtered by event signature. Three checks turn a decoded log into something an application will act on: the source receipt must have succeeded, the emitting address must be the one the contract is bound to, and the log's shape must match what the feed is known to emit.</p>
        <pre><code set:html={emitterCheck}></code></pre>
        <div class="callout">
          <span class="callout-label">A PRACTICAL BINDING NOTE</span>
          <p>Every contract here binds a Chainlink <strong>aggregator</strong>, never the <code>EACAggregatorProxy</code> in front of it. The proxy does not emit <code>AnswerUpdated</code>; binding it would produce a system that deploys cleanly, verifies proofs correctly, and silently receives nothing forever.</p>
          <p>Chain keys carry a similar trap. The published chain list and the live environment have been observed to disagree, so chain keys are read from the chain-info precompile rather than copied from documentation — a wrong key binds the wrong chain and fails only at proof time, long after the code looks correct.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="architecture">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> SECTION 4</span>
        <h2>System architecture</h2>
        <p>Three zones, and the boundary between them is a trust boundary. Ethereum holds the evidence and is never written to. The transport between the chains holds no authority at all. Creditcoin holds every decision that moves value.</p>

