        <h3>Why the transport holds no authority</h3>
        <p>The worker is the only component in the system holding a private key, which makes it the obvious thing to attack. It is worth being precise about what compromising it would achieve. <code>execute</code> is permissionless, so anyone may submit a proof — the worker is a convenience, not a gatekeeper. It moves no value, so a hostile submitter cannot draw principal, release collateral or mint. A forged proof fails at the precompile, and a replayed one fails at the query-id check. The worst outcome available to a compromised worker is declining to submit proofs, which stalls the desk into its own staleness guards: issuance freezes, windows close, and no bad state is written.</p>
        <p>Reads on the other side are similarly narrow. The API returns unsigned calldata for a user's wallet to sign, so the service is not custodial and cannot move a user's position either.</p>
      </section>

