        <h3>What the worker does, and what it refuses to do</h3>
        <p>The worker sweeps each watched chain in windows of fifty blocks, tracking a cursor per watch. For each matching log it records the observation, waits for the source height to be attested, requests the proof, estimates gas and submits. Two behaviours in that loop are worth drawing out because they are consequences of the venue rather than general good practice.</p>
        <p>First, <strong>a decodable revert during gas estimation aborts the submission</strong>. A replayed query, a stale round or an unregistered feed will fail however much gas it is given, and submitting anyway only pays for the revert. Second, because pallet-evm does not always surface precompile reverts during estimation, an <em>indeterminate</em> estimation failure falls back to a size-derived gas limit instead of abandoning a proof that would in fact succeed. Treating both failures identically would either burn gas on certain reverts or silently drop valid proofs.</p>
        <p>The ledger of observed, attested, proved and confirmed events is held in memory, which is a deliberate scope limit rather than a design claim — see Section 10.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="contracts">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> SECTION 6</span>
        <h2>The contract model</h2>
        <p>Six contracts, of which four are Attestcoin Smart Contracts extending <code>ASCBase</code>. The dealing windows are ordinary contracts: they consume a proved round through the oracle's view interface but verify nothing themselves, which keeps the verification surface as small as the set of contracts that actually need it.</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Contract</th><th>Chain</th><th>Base</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {contractRows.map(([name, chain, base, role]) => (
                <tr><td><code>{name}</code></td><td>{chain}</td><td>{base}</td><td>{role}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>Supply bound to reserves</h3>
        <p>The issuance guard is the clearest statement of the whole design, and it is four lines long. <code>provedReserves</code> moves only inside <code>_processAndEmitEvent</code>, which runs only after the precompile has verified a round from the bound Proof-of-Reserve aggregator.</p>
        <pre><code set:html={issueGuard}></code></pre>
        <p>The issuer holds <code>onlyOwner</code> and can therefore choose to mint less, never more. The ceiling is a figure they do not control and cannot forge, and with no proof at all the token cannot be issued even once.</p>
        <h4>Units are matched on purpose</h4>
        <p>Tokens are denominated in the reserve feed's own unit — grams for both listed metals — so the safety-critical comparison <code>totalSupply() + amount &lt;= provedReserves</code> needs no unit conversion. Gram-to-troy-ounce arithmetic exists only in the pricing path inside <code>MetalWindow</code>, using a troy ounce of 31.1034768 g scaled by 10<sup>7</sup> to stay in integer arithmetic. The trade-off is deliberate: a rounding error in pricing costs basis points on a fill, whereas a rounding error in the supply cap breaks the invariant the token exists to enforce.</p>

        <h3>The credit lifecycle</h3>
        <p><code>ASCRepoDesk</code> moves an agreement through five states. Two transitions are gated on proofs from a source chain, one on a proof plus a mark-to-market check, and two on conditions the chain can evaluate for itself.</p>

