      <section class="chapter" id="listing">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> SECTION 7</span>
        <h2>Listing a real-world asset</h2>
        <p>Silver was listed after gold without a line of new contract code. That is the test of whether the model generalises, and the path has four steps.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>The asset needs a Proof-of-Reserve feed on an attested chain.</strong> This is the gate, and it is deliberately the hardest step. The feed is trustworthy precisely because the issuer does not operate it; an issuer who cannot obtain independent reserve attestation cannot list here, which is the intended outcome rather than a limitation to engineer around.</li>
          <li><strong>Deploy <code>ProvedMetal</code></strong> with its name, symbol and that aggregator. The feed is immutable in the constructor, so supply is capped by proved reserves permanently and irrevocably from the first block.</li>
          <li><strong>Register its price feed</strong> with <code>registerAggregator(...)</code> on the existing <code>ProvedPriceOracle</code> — one transaction, no deployment. Because a proved round is keyed by its emitting aggregator, one oracle serves every listed asset.</li>
          <li><strong>Deploy a <code>MetalWindow</code></strong> against that aggregator to make the asset tradeable, and call <code>registerCollateralAsset(...)</code> on the desk with its advance rate and maintenance margin to make it borrowable.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Adding the feeds to the worker's watch list and the asset to the dashboard's listings is configuration, held as JSON in the deployment environment rather than in code. The worker picks up the new aggregator on restart and begins proving its rounds.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <span class="callout-label">WHAT GENERALISES, AND WHAT DOES NOT</span>
          <p>The listing path is asset-agnostic in its mechanics but not in its units. A metal denominated in grams against a feed that reports grams needs no conversion in the supply cap; an asset whose reserve feed reports in a different unit from its token would reintroduce arithmetic into the safety-critical path. The honest generalisation is therefore: any asset with an independent Proof-of-Reserve feed can be listed, provided its token is denominated in that feed's unit.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="chapter" id="risk">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> SECTION 8</span>
        <h2>Risk parameters</h2>
        <p>Every value below was read from the live testnet deployment rather than from configuration files, since a deployed parameter and an intended one are not the same claim.</p>
