              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3>How the advance rate is enforced</h3>
        <p>At <code>drawPrincipal</code>, collateral value is computed from the pledged amount and the last proved round, the advance rate caps the permitted principal, and a draw above that cap reverts. The realised loan-to-value is computed on-chain and emitted with the draw, so it is an observed figure rather than a reported one:</p>
        <pre><code>ltvBps = (principalUsd * 10_000) / collateralValueUsd</code></pre>
        <p>In the worked example of Section 9, $20,000 was drawn against ten troy ounces marked at $4,349.135 — $43,491.35 of bullion — giving 4,598 bp, or 45.98%, comfortably inside the 6,000 bp advance rate.</p>
        <h3>On the choice of 26 hours</h3>
        <p>Both metal price feeds and both Proof-of-Reserve feeds publish on a 24-hour heartbeat. A staleness bound equal to the heartbeat would trip on ordinary jitter; two hours of slack absorbs that without materially widening the exposure. The bound is a hard stop in both directions — past it, issuance reverts with <code>StaleReserves</code> and dealing windows close with <code>StalePrice</code> rather than filling on a figure nobody has re-proved.</p>
      </section>

