      <section class="chapter" id="limits">
        <span class="eyebrow"><i></i> SECTION 10</span>
        <h2>What this design does not prove</h2>
        <p>This section is the one to read adversarially. A proof system oversold is worse than one honestly bounded, because the overselling is what makes people stop checking.</p>
        <ul class="limits">
          <li><strong>It proves provenance, not truth.</strong> The system proves that a named Chainlink aggregator published a particular number in a particular block. It does not prove the number is correct. What it removes from the trust path is the relay and the issuer's self-report; the <em>auditor</em> behind a Proof-of-Reserve feed remains trusted, as does Chainlink's operation of the feed. Anyone describing this as trustless reserve verification is overstating it.</li>
          <li><strong>The demonstration borrows someone else's reserve feeds.</strong> <code>KAU Reserves</code> and <code>KAG Reserves</code> attest Kinesis's vaults. The listings therefore model an issuer whose reserves those feeds report — a stand-in, in the same way <code>TestBullion</code> stands in for a production bullion token. In a real deployment an issuer commissions their own Proof-of-Reserve feed and only the bound aggregator address changes; no contract logic does.</li>
          <li><strong>There is no CTC/USD Chainlink feed.</strong> Quoting the dealing windows in a USD token is forced rather than chosen: no provable way to price metal in tCTC exists today. Credit is drawn in tCTC, but the mark and the quote are USD-denominated, and closing that gap needs a feed that does not currently exist.</li>
          <li><strong>The direction is read-only.</strong> Attestcoin's writability path was in third-party audit at the time of writing, so Creditcoin never writes to Ethereum. Releasing source-chain collateral is a source-chain decision whose event is then proved back to Creditcoin — the desk observes the release, it does not command it.</li>
          <li><strong>Trust binds to an emitting address, not to a chain.</strong> <code>ASCBase.execute</code> does not pass <code>chainKey</code> through to the handler, so each contract checks that a proved log came from the address it is bound to, following the reference examples. A bound address on a different attested chain would satisfy that check. This is a known property of the current base contract, not a mitigation the applications implement.</li>
          <li><strong>Prices are only as fresh as the last proved round.</strong> A sharp move between rounds is invisible to the desk until the next round is proved. The 26-hour guard bounds how stale a mark can be before the system stops acting on it; it does not make the mark continuous, and a lender should read the advance rate as compensation for that discreteness.</li>
          <li><strong>Operational scope is a demonstration's scope.</strong> Exactly one worker may run — two would prove the same events twice and pay for the duplicate's revert. Watches sweep sequentially, so a large backfill on one chain delays the others. The proof ledger is held in memory and does not survive a restart; it is an observability aid, and no contract state depends on it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>None of these undermine the three claims of Section 1, and it is worth being precise about why. Each claim is of the form "the contract will not act unless a proof of the form it expects verifies in the same transaction." That property holds under every limit above. What the limits bound is the <em>meaning</em> of the proved fact — whose vault, whose audit, how fresh — rather than the enforcement of the gate.</p>
      </section>

